      "text": " <think>\nTo determine the correct ranking of Xiao Wang's four subjects from high to low, we need to analyze the given conditions step by step:\n\n1. **Condition 1**: The sum of the scores of political and professional subject one is equal to the sum of the scores of the other two subjects.\n   - Let's denote the scores as follows:\n     - Political: \\( P \\)\n     - English: \\( E \\)\n     - Major one: \\( M_1 \\)\n     - Major two: \\( M_2 \\)\n   - This condition can be written as:\n     \\[\n     P + M_1 = E + M_2\n     \\]\n\n2. **Condition 2**: The sum of the scores of the political and professional subjects two is greater than the sum of the scores of the other two subjects.\n   - This condition can be written as:\n     \\[\n     P + M_2 > E + M_1\n     \\]\n\n3. **Condition 3**: The scores of the professional subject one are higher than the sum of the scores of the political and English subjects.\n   - This condition can be written as:\n     \\[\n     M_1 > P + E\n     \\]\n\nNow, let's analyze these conditions to find the correct ranking.\n\n### Step-by-Step Analysis:\n\n1. **Condition 1**:\n   \\[\n   P + M_1 = E + M_2\n   \\]\n   This implies that the sum of the scores of political and major one is equal to the sum of the scores of English and major two.\n\n2. **Condition 2**:\n   \\[\n   P + M_2 > E + M_1\n   \\]\n   This implies that the sum of the scores of political and major two is greater than the sum of the scores of English and major one.\n\n3. **Condition 3**:\n   \\[\n   M_1 > P + E\n   \\]\n   This implies that the score of major one is greater than the sum of the scores of political and English.\n\n### Combining Conditions:\n\nFrom Condition 3, we know:\n\\[\nM_1 > P + E\n\\]\n\nFrom Condition 1, we know:\n\\[\nP + M_1 = E + M_2\n\\]\n\nFrom Condition 2, we know:\n\\[\nP + M_2 > E + M_1\n\\]\n\nLet's substitute \\( P + M_1 \\) from Condition 1 into Condition 2:\n\\[\nP + M_2 > E + (E + M_2)\n\\]\n\\[\nP + M_2 > 2E + M_2\n\\]\n\\[\nP > 2E\n\\]\n\nNow, we have:\n\\[\nM_1 > P + E\n\\]\n\\[\nP > 2E\n\\]\n\nSince \\( M_1 > P + E \\) and \\( P > 2E \\), it follows that:\n\\[\nM_1 > P + E > 2E\n\\]\n\nThis implies that \\( M_1 \\) is the highest score among the four subjects.\n\nNext, we need to compare \\( M_2 \\) with \\( E \\) and \\( P \\). From Condition 1:\n\\[\nP + M_1 = E + M_2\n\\]\nSince \\( M_1 \\) is the highest score, \\( E + M_2 \\) must be less than \\( P + M_1 \\). Therefore, \\( M_2 \\) must be less than \\( P \\).\n\nFinally, we need to compare \\( E \\) with \\( P \\). From Condition 3:\n\\[\nM_1 > P + E\n\\]\nSince \\( M_1 \\) is the highest score, \\( P + E \\) must be less than \\( M_1 \\). Therefore, \\( E \\) must be less than \\( P \\).\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nBased on the analysis, the correct ranking from high to low is:\n\\[\nM_1 > P > E > M_2\n\\]\n\nThus, the correct option is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{C}\n\\]\n</think>\n<answer>\nC) Major subject one, major subject two, politics, english.\n</answer>",
